  "full_response": "SQL (Structured Query Language) and NoSQL (Not Only SQL) databases are two broad categories of database systems, each designed to handle different types of data and use cases. Below is a detailed comparison, including examples of when to use each.\n\n---\n\n### **1. SQL Databases (Relational Databases)**\nSQL databases are **relational**, meaning they store data in **tables** with **rows and columns**. They use a predefined **schema** (structure) and enforce **relationships** between tables using **foreign keys**.\n\n#### **Key Characteristics:**\n- **Structured data**: Data must fit into a predefined schema (columns with specific data types).\n- **ACID compliance**: Ensures **Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability** (e.g., transactions are reliable).\n- **SQL language**: Uses **Structured Query Language (SQL)** for querying and managing data.\n- **Vertical scaling**: Scales by increasing hardware power (CPU, RAM, SSD).\n- **Joins**: Supports complex queries with `JOIN` operations across tables.\n\n#### **Examples of SQL Databases:**\n- **MySQL** (open-source, widely used in web apps)\n- **PostgreSQL** (advanced features, extensible)\n- **Microsoft SQL Server** (enterprise-grade)\n- **Oracle Database** (high-performance, enterprise)\n- **SQLite** (lightweight, embedded in mobile apps)\n\n#### **When to Use SQL Databases?**\n✅ **Structured data with clear relationships** (e.g., financial systems, inventory management).\n✅ **Need for complex queries** (e.g., reporting, analytics with `JOINs`).\n✅ **Transactions are critical** (e.g., banking, e-commerce orders).\n✅ **Data integrity is a priority** (e.g., healthcare records, legal systems).\n\n#### **Example Use Case:**\n- **E-commerce Platform** (e.g., Amazon)\n  - **Users** table (user_id, name, email)\n  - **Orders** table (order_id, user_id, date, total)\n  - **Products** table (product_id, name, price)\n  - **Order_Items** table (order_id, product_id, quantity)\n  - **Why SQL?** Need to enforce relationships (e.g., an order must belong to a user) and run complex queries (e.g., \"Show all orders for a user in the last month\").\n\n---\n\n### **2. NoSQL",
